this is the only route into agalia  the town at the heart of a war between  two cartels in michoacan southwest  mexico  no journalists have been here since the  jalisco new generation cartel  advanced to take the land around it  we've been told to take this dirt track  over the mountains to get in but it's  not simple  we've just seen in quick succession a  checkpoint that we had to pass through  with  armed guys by the side of the road a  burnt out  truck and then the letters uh the  initials of the jalisco new generation  cartel actually scrawled on the road  itself  there's no real doubt about whose  territory we're in  when we pass the second checkpoint they  want to talk to us  we don't have any necessity to rob we're  drug traffickers  it's true that we're in a fight to  struggle but we're fighting for the  rights of each of us  and for the people  they're on a propaganda push to go with  their advance into this territory  they say they'll let through civilians  who dare to travel  finally we make it to the town they're  fighting for agaliya  on the outskirts it looks like a ghost  town  it might well be one soon thousands have  fled the violence and isolation here  those left have no one to help them we  didn't see a single policeman on our way  or a soldier despite the fact the army  actually has a barracks in the town  residents say they rarely patrol  near the center are signs of life la  paria taqueria  has been serving the village for two  decades but maria contreras  says it's hard to keep it going  it's affecting us a lot everything's got  more expensive  it's really hard to bring in supplies  that's because the main road to the  nearest city  of patsyngan has been closed by another  group carteles unidos  they're fighting the jalisco cartel and  have the area under siege  petrol has to be smuggled in the garage  is out of action  gas is scarce so is medicine and bottled  water  farmers say they can't get their crops  or cattle to market  and the siege has had other costs when  daniela and gabriella's granddad had a  burst stomach ulcer  they couldn't get him to the hospital on  the other side the blockade  he died i felt so helpless because we  couldn't do anything it was out of our  hands  and angry too it's not right that we're  living like this  and he wasn't the only one at the same  time a lady had a miscarriage because  she was trying to get to the hospital  and couldn't we heard more stories  townspeople killed in the crossfire  between the cartels on the road  or in the town itself there's a nightly  soundtrack here  you can probably hear that that's the  sound of gunfire we're just outside of  the village church  night has only just fallen and they've  started shooting here  and where are the authorities absent say  the town's people  until a surprise visit by the governor  last week just as election season began  but when a teacher dared to protest to  him he did this  it enraged many in agalia when we  visited  they gathered to demand one thing  number one free transit on the road from  aguilia  to apating and security for those  traveling  the state security minister also came by  while we were visiting to promise just  that  but he didn't talk to the people of  agalia instead he held a press  conference outside the town  the media he brought with him we joined  them  and i asked him repeatedly if he was  aware of the criminal checkpoints  well if you tell me where if you pass me  the coordinates when we finish  he denied knowing of them the next day  we tried to leave passing burnt out  empty towns  a sign perhaps of agolia's future but we  couldn't get through to a passing game  it's not even 24 hours since the  secretary of public security came down  this road and said that it was now  open and there would be free transit we  just tried to get down it  we've already heard that it's blocked so  we're having to go back down to agalia  eventually the police filled in the  trench that one of the gangs had dug  across  it that night the criminals dug it out  again  the highways closed once more the siege  and suffering continues  john holman al jazeera agalia
 

